# Gate step 1084-nzqc — data, not code.
#
# Binds the fixture for the two escapes that made the SIGPIPE verdict guard
# blind. Hermetic: each arm builds a throwaway git repo and drives the guard
# through TILLANDSIAS_SIGPIPE_ROOT/TILLANDSIAS_SIGPIPE_BASE, so no arm reads
# this checkout's diff — a diff-scoped guard let loose on the real working tree
# would assert about whatever the author happened to be editing.
#
# FIVE OF THE EIGHT ARMS ARE NEGATIVE CONTROLS, and that ratio is the point: the
# fix WIDENS a guard, so the risk is not that it keeps missing things but that
# it starts refusing correct code. An EOF-reading consumer, a bounded producer,
# a sigpipe-ok marker on a folded line, an unchanged file, and an untouched line
# in a CHANGED file must all stay unflagged. The last two are separate subjects:
# one pins file-level scoping, the other line-level, and a mutation proved a
# single arm could not reach both.
#
# STEP_SCRIPT is a LITERAL path (1063-nraf).
STEP_DESC="Checking the SIGPIPE verdict guard sees absolute paths and continued pipelines (1084-nzqc)"
STEP_SCRIPT="scripts/test-sigpipe-guard-sees-paths-and-continuations.sh"
STEP_ERROR="the SIGPIPE verdict guard regressed (1084-nzqc) — either an absolute-path producer or a pipeline split across line continuations is escaping it again (the 1069-c9w6 fingerprint), or the widened matcher has started refusing correct code; see the verdict line above"
STEP_OK="SIGPIPE verdict guard path/continuation check passed"
# Exit 3 means the guard itself is absent — nothing was examined.
STEP_SKIP_EXIT=3
STEP_SKIP_DESC="scripts/check-sigpipe-verdict-pipelines-added.sh is absent, so neither escape could be exercised"
STEP_SECOND_REGIME="macneo 2026-09-22 darwin ok:   C2 (negative control): a pipeline with no branch on its status is NOT flagged — the guard tests for a ve"
